From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bartosz Nitka <niteria@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 18:48:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/apple: m4: power the internal DCP and its domains
 down while blanked

On DPMS off the J713 internal display gets setPowerState(0) (A473),
which stops its pipe, but the DCP keeps running and its CPU, frontend and
system power domains stay on. macOS goes further for display sleep: it
releases the AP link, idles RTKit, stops the DCP CPU and powers the three
domains off, and on wake starts the CPU again on the retained firmware.

Do the same once the display has been blanked for appledrm.power_gate_ms
(0, the default, never), and before system sleep while blanked. First,
changing nothing, ask every provider whether it can take part: both
display DARTs, the PMP vote, the PMC interface and the PMGR states. If
one cannot, the display just stays logically off. Then:

  A500, drain the callbacks, apple_rtkit_idle() and hold RX, stop the CPU
  (after its idle status), take in anything the firmware sent meanwhile
  (anything there fails the session, before a power-down), power down
  both display DARTs (streams off, power-down flush, clock protection
  off), release the PMP DISP vote, clear the PMC interface enables and
  power the consumer-switched CPU, frontend and system states off.

The first frame afterwards reverses it before setPowerState(1), in
macOS's order: domains, PMP vote, PMC enables, DARTs (state checked,
streams back), then RTKit's
retained wake, which restarts the CPU and gets HELLO, the endpoint map
and IOP/AP ON without INIT, and the display preparation macOS sends
(A414, A412). A frame cancels a power-down not yet started; a power-down
under way finishes first. While the domains are down no register of the
DCP, its mailbox or its DARTs is used: health checks, status and resume
leave them alone, and resume leaves the display down until the next frame.
Any failure fails the session, as other DCP failures do, but none refuses
system sleep: the power-down is a saving, not a condition of sleep.
Setting power_gate_ms to 0 also stops a power-down already scheduled.

Only an internal J713 DCP whose node names its PMC interface
(apple,pmc-interface) does this. debugfs iomfb_m4 shows the gate state,
the number of power-downs and the last power-down and power-up times.

On a J713 with power_gate_ms=3000, 204 power-downs and power-ups in one
boot completed without an error, one of them started by a suspend 1.5 s
after the blank: the display domains read back level 0 while off, stayed
off across suspend-to-idle, and the display came back with a completed
frame each time. Awake with DPMS off it draws about 15 mW less than
setPowerState(0) alone (8 alternating pairs); one pair of
suspend-to-idle runs differed by about 10 mW. It is a prerequisite for
deeper SoC sleep with the display off.

Host test: m4-dpms-retirement covers the power-down scheduling, its
absence when not enabled, the power-up before setPowerState(1) and a
power-up that fails; m4-power-gate the order of both sequences, a
provider that refuses (nothing changes), a late firmware message (the
session fails before any power-down) and power_gate_ms turned off after
the blank.

Signed-off-by: Bartosz Nitka <niteria@gmail.com>
(cherry picked from commit 849f12897aa3d78d25a7735f722c4e82c28aa011)
[j604: k610 drm sync: picked from Aurora PR #1059 (aurora/pr-1059, base
 05ae9bf1be, two iomfb_m4.c commits behind 50d5865ea6) instead of the
 a15y hand-port 0a9cac712e, since the synced iomfb_m4.c is upstream's.
 One conflict, in __dcp_m4_runtime_health(): the a15y health-cost split
 (25f1aec9a1 port) keeps its laps and the separate internal-only PIODMA
 check, and the gated early return goes first; dcp_m4_runtime_health()
 now zeroes part[] so a powered-down DCP leaves no stale lap values.
 selftests m4.py/run.py are not carried (not in a15y; as in k609-dcp).
 J713 gate kept here (gate_capable = !external && apple_pmp_j713_board()
 && apple,pmc-interface); the J604 widening is the next commit.
 Default unchanged: power_gate_ms=0 (0644).]

[dylan: carried from aurora-silicon/linux 849f12897aa3; one co-author trailer of the original message is not reproduced here, see the upstream commit for the full text]
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c
index 305d36cac3..3f12a7e869 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/iomfb_m4.c
@@ -1245,7 +1245,8 @@ static void dcp_m4_health_account(struct dcp_m4_runtime *r, u64 ns, const u64 *p
 /* Callers hold dcp->lock; the counters are statistics for the log and debugfs. */
 static int dcp_m4_runtime_health(struct dcp_m4_runtime *r)
 {
-	u64 part[ARRAY_SIZE(r->health_max_part_ns)];
+	/* Zero: a powered-down DCP returns before any part is timed. */
+	u64 part[ARRAY_SIZE(r->health_max_part_ns)] = {};
 	ktime_t start = ktime_get();
 	int ret = __dcp_m4_runtime_health(r, part);
 
